  "subtitle": "the world's first talking camera, a 35mm point-and-shoot that audibly coaches the photographer",
  "why_scout_cares": "Every speech device in the museum either takes your words in (Covox, DragonDictate) or reads them out (TRS-80 Voice Synthesizer, Speak & Spell). The Minolta Talker does something neither: it watches the world through its own sensors and SPEAKS instructions back at you mid-gesture. Point the camera, half-press the shutter, and a female voice tells you what to fix — 'Too dark, use flash.' 'Check distance.' 'Load film.' The shutter fires regardless, so the voice is a pure advisory interface layer laid over a physical craft, closing a loop that a novice photographer can actually act on. It is a mainstream consumer camera (about $130 in 1984), it sold as the 'Talkman' in Japan, and it is genuinely weird in a way the museum exists to preserve: a machine that nags you, kindly, in the middle of your hands.",
